ity cards yet)\n- **Current Status**: Leading overall (+3,500 cumulative)\n\n## Hand Strength Evaluation\n\n**K♥ J♦ (King-Jack offsuit)** is a **medium-strength starting hand** in heads-up Texas Hold'em:\n\n### Positives:\n- Two high cards (Broadway cards)\n- Can make top pair with good kicker\n- Straight potential (Q-10-9 or A-Q-10)\n- Reasonable equity against random hands (~60% vs random)\n\n### Negatives:\n- Offsuit (no flush potential with both cards)\n- Vulnerable to premium pairs (AA, KK, QQ, JJ)\n- Dominated by AK, AJ\n- Only medium strength - not premium\n\n## Strategic Considerations\n\n1. **Early in tournament** (hand 3/20): Can play more aggressively, plenty of room to recover\n2. **Stack sizes even**: No significant chip advantage/disadvantage this hand\n3. **Check option available**: Suggests I'm in Big Blind position or no one has bet yet\n4. **Heads-up dynamics**: Hand values increase significantly in 2-player format\n\n## Evaluation of Options\n\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Too weak - K-J is playable in heads-up\n2. **Check (0)**: Safe, see the flop cheaply, but gives up initiative\n3. **Raise (200+)**: Shows strength, builds pot with decent hand, takes control\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Too aggressive for K-J offsuit pre-flop\n\n## Reason\nK-J offsuit is a **solid playable hand in heads-up poker** but not strong enough to commit all chips pre-flop. A moderate raise of **200 chips** (standard 2x big blind raise) accomplishes multiple goals:\n- Tests opponent's hand strength\n- Builds pot with a decent hand\n- Maintains pot control\n- Preserves stack for post-flop play\n- Shows aggression without overcommitting\n\nChecking is too passive and surrenders initiative. A raise puts pressure on the opponent and allows me to potentially win immediately or see a favorable flop with position and control.\n\n## Answer\n200"}
