ium pocket pair\n- Board: 2♠ K♦ J♥ (Flop)\n- My chips: 19,750\n- I'm ahead in cumulative wins (+2,300)\n- This is hand 11/20\n\n**Hand Strength Analysis:**\n- I have a pair of 9s, which is currently third pair (below the King and Jack on board)\n- My hand is vulnerable to:\n  - Anyone holding K or J (top pair or second pair would beat me)\n  - Anyone holding a pocket pair higher than 9s (TT, JJ, QQ, KK, AA)\n  - Potential straight draws (Q-T would need an A or 9)\n\n**Opponent's Actions:**\n- Pre-flop: Called my raise of 200 (showing some interest but not re-raising)\n- Could have: High cards (AK, KQ, KJ, etc.), medium pairs, suited connectors, or drawing hands\n\n**Position & Pot Control:**\n- I raised pre-flop and opponent just called\n- On the flop, I can check to see what opponent does, or make a continuation bet\n- Checking shows weakness but gains information\n- Betting (continuation bet) maintains aggression and might take down the pot immediately\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- With 9 hands remaining and a decent chip lead, I don't need to risk everything here\n- A pair of 9s on this board is marginal - not strong enough to commit heavily, but decent enough to contest\n- A small continuation bet (around 300-400) would be reasonable to test opponent's strength\n- If opponent shows aggression, I can fold without major damage\n- Checking is passive but could be dangerous if opponent has position and bets\n\nReason: \n\nI'll make a moderate continuation bet of 400 chips. This serves multiple purposes: (1) It represents about 2/3 of the pot, maintaining pressure as the pre-flop aggressor, (2) It's small enough that I'm not overcommitting with a marginal hand, (3) It gives me information about opponent's hand strength, and (4) If opponent folds, I take down the pot without showdown. Given that I raised pre-flop, checking here would look weak and invite aggression. A bet of 400 is large enough to be meaningful but small enough to abandon if I face resistance.\n